WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.065
Sensitive files include more than the
obvious key or credential file.

2
00:00:04.145 --> 00:00:07.227
Configuration files, exported reports,
cached responses,

3
00:00:07.307 --> 00:00:10.329
diagnostic bundles, generated archives,
request traces,

4
00:00:10.409 --> 00:00:14.639
and logs may contain secrets, tokens,
identifiers, private data, internal paths,

5
00:00:14.719 --> 00:00:15.867
or operational state.

6
00:00:15.947 --> 00:00:18.002
If these files are created by a C program,

7
00:00:18.082 --> 00:00:20.378
their permissions need explicit attention.

8
00:00:20.458 --> 00:00:22.694
Logs are especially easy to underestimate.

9
00:00:22.774 --> 00:00:26.340
A useful log can become a data exposure
path if it records credentials,

10
00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:29.804
request bodies, private filenames,
internal directory layouts,

11
00:00:29.884 --> 00:00:31.878
or detailed identifiers without need.

12
00:00:31.958 --> 00:00:36.369
Logging should support troubleshooting
while respecting the sensitivity of the data

13
00:00:36.449 --> 00:00:37.235
being written.

14
00:00:37.315 --> 00:00:40.820
Temporary output should be protected like
any other sensitive file.

15
00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:43.861
It may exist briefly, but briefly is not
the same as safely.

16
00:00:43.941 --> 00:00:46.540
Other processes, backup jobs, indexing
services,

17
00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:49.339
or operators may see a temporary file
while it exists.

18
00:00:49.419 --> 00:00:53.166
If the contents are sensitive, creation
permissions, location, naming,

19
00:00:53.246 --> 00:00:54.454
and cleanup all matter.

20
00:00:54.534 --> 00:00:58.644
Generated files should not receive broader
permissions than the data requires.

21
00:00:58.724 --> 00:01:02.652
A report generated from private records
does not become public because it was

22
00:01:02.732 --> 00:01:03.940
produced by a batch job.

23
00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:07.646
A cache of sensitive responses does not
become low risk because it can be

24
00:01:07.726 --> 00:01:08.451
regenerated.

25
00:01:08.531 --> 00:01:12.943
Permission decisions should follow the
content, not the convenience of the workflow.

26
00:01:13.023 --> 00:01:14.594
Cleanup is part of protection.

27
00:01:14.674 --> 00:01:18.119
Sensitive temporary files should be
removed when no longer needed,

28
00:01:18.199 --> 00:01:21.764
and cleanup should be narrow enough to
avoid touching unrelated data.

29
00:01:21.844 --> 00:01:26.255
Security review should include these
secondary outputs because they often become the

30
00:01:26.335 --> 00:01:29.780
place where sensitive data escapes the
intended protection model.
